course: dictionary
lesson: 9003
title: One word, two jobs
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words can be a thing and an action. Ski is one of them. A ski is a long, narrow board for sliding over snow. To ski is the action of moving over snow on skis. The words around it show which job it is doing.
explain: «Water, sand and snow reflect UV, so you can burn even under an umbrella or on a ski slope.» Here ski is part of a place name. A ski slope is a snowy hill where people ski, so the words slope and snow tell you what ski means here.
explain: In London, Anya mentions skiing in Switzerland and a girl says, «Stop flexing» («flexing» means showing off). The -ing ending on skiing shows an action, like running or swimming. Check each use by asking: is this a thing, or something people do?
fact: A ski is one of a pair of long, narrow, flat boards fixed to boots for sliding over snow.
fact: To ski means to move over snow on skis.
fact: Ski can be a noun (a ski, skis) or a verb (ski, skis, skied, skiing).
fact: Useful phrases: go skiing, ski slope, ski lift, ski resort, water ski.
fact: In PE and safety lessons, pupils learn to wear a helmet and stay on marked runs.
exercise: In «Anya mentions skiing in Switzerland», what is skiing? | Painting a picture | Swimming in a lake | *Moving over snow on skis | Climbing a tall tree
exercise: «You can burn even on a ski slope.» What is a ski slope? | *A snowy hill where people ski | A lake with boats on it | A shop that sells ice cream | A kind of ski boot
exercise: Which sentence uses ski as a thing? | She skied very fast | *Pack the skis in the car | We are skiing down the hill | They ski every winter
test: Yesterday Sam ___ down the big hill on his new pair of skis. Which word fills the gap? | ski | skiing | *skied | skis
test: «Water, sand and snow reflect UV, so you can burn even under an umbrella or on a ski slope.» Why can you burn on a ski slope? | Umbrellas make you burn | Slopes are always in the shade | Skis are very hot | *Snow reflects UV light
exam: Which word means more than one ski? | skiing | *skis | skied | ski
